WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.421
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:07.521 --> 00:00:22.089
Welcome Superstars in History, the talk show featuring interviews with famous people from the past.

3
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:30.434
I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest was a fabulous scientist and inventor whose best-known invention is

4
00:00:30.474 --> 00:00:34.396
something that I personally could never live without.

5
00:00:35.264 --> 00:00:41.350
Please welcome the father of the telephone, Alexander Graham Bell!

6
00:00:58.746 --> 00:01:00.368
Thank you for calling, Faye.

7
00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.281
And thank you for inventing the telephone, Alexander!

8
00:01:06.061 --> 00:01:11.643
You were always interested in sound and communication, even as a child, right?

9
00:01:12.563 --> 00:01:13.784
Yes, that's correct.

10
00:01:14.684 --> 00:01:17.785
I was born in Scotland in 1847.

11
00:01:18.765 --> 00:01:24.087
My father was a speech teacher, so I grew up learning about all the sounds the human voice

12
00:01:24.187 --> 00:01:24.647
can make.

13
00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:31.466
And I loved to apply my knowledge in amusing ways—I was quite a good mimic, and I taught

14
00:01:31.646 --> 00:01:33.008
our terrier to speak.

15
00:01:34.168 --> 00:01:34.969
Excuse me?

16
00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.632
You taught your dog to say actual words?

17
00:01:39.793 --> 00:01:44.597
While he growled, I moved his throat and mouth around to make it sound as if he were

18
00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:49.541
speaking. He could "say" things like, "How are you, Grandmama?"

19
00:01:50.041 --> 00:01:51.863
Visitors adored this trick!

20
00:01:53.944 --> 00:01:59.788
But on a much more serious note, my interest in sound also arose from my mother's situation.

21
00:02:00.849 --> 00:02:06.853
She was a gifted pianist, so I was greatly distressed when she started to lose her hearing.

22
00:02:07.854 --> 00:02:11.297
I was always dreaming of ways to improve her hearing.

23
00:02:12.397 --> 00:02:16.120
It sounds like you were an imaginative, sensitive kid.

24
00:02:17.021 --> 00:02:19.182
Did you invent anything in childhood?

25
00:02:20.143 --> 00:02:25.866
At age 12 I invented a de-husking machine for a neighbor who owned a flour mill.

26
00:02:26.706 --> 00:02:31.028
It quickly removed the husk from the wheat, saving a great amount of time.

27
00:02:31.868 --> 00:02:36.410
And my brother and I created a talking machine that was shaped like a human skull.

28
00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:40.352
It had rubber cheeks and lips and a tin voice box.

29
00:02:41.152 --> 00:02:45.834
To make the machine talk, we pumped air through a tube that served as its windpipe.

30
00:02:46.874 --> 00:02:50.556
It was clever, but I'm afraid it wasn't particularly useful!

31
00:02:51.508 --> 00:03:00.573
You attended university in Scotland, and in 1868, you began teaching the deaf in London while doing experiments

32
00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:02.394
with sound in your spare time.

33
00:03:03.375 --> 00:03:08.818
But then in 1870, you suddenly moved to Canada.

34
00:03:08.838 --> 00:03:13.381
Why? Sadly, my two brothers died from tuberculosis.

35
00:03:14.301 --> 00:03:19.144
I was ill too, so my parents wanted me to move to Canada for fresh country air.

36
00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.102
While I recovered, I continued my sound experiments and study of the human voice.

37
00:03:25.943 --> 00:03:32.465
By 1871, I was well enough to move to Boston, to train teachers in Visible Speech.

38
00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:38.308
This was a writing system invented by my father to help the deaf and mute learn to speak.

39
00:03:39.288 --> 00:03:41.269
I also began teaching deaf students.

40
00:03:42.289 --> 00:03:44.830
Oh, and I fell in love and got married.

41
00:03:45.831 --> 00:03:48.672
My wife, Mabel, also happened to be deaf.

42
00:03:49.864 --> 00:03:57.070
Your work on sound and speech was so personal; it probably influenced your ideas for your inventions.

43
00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.592
When did you start working on the telephone?

44
00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:07.998
Like other inventors in the 1870s, I was particularly fascinated with the idea of improving the telegraph.

45
00:04:08.899 --> 00:04:14.543
It was a popular device for sending messages, but only one message could be transmitted across the wire

46
00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:15.084
at a time.

47
00:04:15.916 --> 00:04:20.478
This made the service slow, yet demand for faster telegraphs was growing rapidly.

48
00:04:21.278 --> 00:04:24.660
Inventors realized this was a tremendous opportunity!

49
00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.101
How did you think you could improve the telegraph?

50
00:04:29.022 --> 00:04:34.664
I had hoped to invent a harmonic telegraph that could transmit sounds of different pitches across a single

51
00:04:34.724 --> 00:04:37.405
wire, to send multiple messages at once.

52
00:04:38.265 --> 00:04:41.907
But I didn't know enough about electricity to make my ideas work.

53
00:04:42.827 --> 00:04:47.710
So I found an assistant, an electrician and mechanic named Thomas Watson.

54
00:04:48.511 --> 00:04:51.873
How did your telegraph turn into the telephone?

55
00:04:52.994 --> 00:04:54.655
That was a lucky accident.

56
00:04:55.496 --> 00:05:00.639
One day in 1875, while we were working on the telegraph, a transmitter jammed.

57
00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.464
When Watson tried to fix it, a part vibrated, making a twang that traveled along the wire.

58
00:05:08.244 --> 00:05:13.468
This made me wonder about the possibility of transmitting the human voice across a wire.

59
00:05:14.332 --> 00:05:16.853
Intrigued, we experimented further.

60
00:05:17.774 --> 00:05:24.857
At last, in March of 1876, I spoke into the mouthpiece of our electric speaking device: "Mr.

61
00:05:24.917 --> 00:05:27.318
Watson, come here—I want to see you!"

62
00:05:28.078 --> 00:05:29.238
And he heard me!

63
00:05:31.339 --> 00:05:36.201
My voice had traveled across the wire and through a speaker at the other end!

64
00:05:37.442 --> 00:05:46.661
Your telephone patent is one of most valuable ones in history—but there's some controversy over who really invented

65
00:05:46.681 --> 00:05:47.402
the telephone.

66
00:05:48.642 --> 00:05:49.142
Ah, yes.

67
00:05:49.582 --> 00:05:57.965
Electronic devices were a very new technology, and several inventors, like Antonio Meucci and Elisha Gray, were also

68
00:05:58.025 --> 00:05:59.945
working on communication devices.

69
00:06:00.886 --> 00:06:03.687
We each had patents for similar technologies.

70
00:06:04.627 --> 00:06:08.188
The question of who invented what and when is complicated.

71
00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:14.792
In fact, hundreds of court cases were brought against my company, the Bell Telephone Company.

72
00:06:15.773 --> 00:06:18.335
In the end, though, my patents were upheld.

73
00:06:19.516 --> 00:06:21.057
Let's get back to your achievements.

74
00:06:21.897 --> 00:06:26.961
In 1880 the French government awarded you the Volta Prize.

75
00:06:27.861 --> 00:06:29.102
What did you do next?

76
00:06:30.163 --> 00:06:36.888
I set up Volta Laboratory, an institution for experiments and research to improve the lives of the deaf.

77
00:06:38.172 --> 00:06:46.034
I also invented the photophone, a communication device that transmits speech messages using light beams instead of wires.

78
00:06:47.075 --> 00:06:53.356
People always made a fuss about the telephone, but I suspected the photophone would be recognized one day

79
00:06:53.497 --> 00:06:55.857
as a rather important invention too.

80
00:06:57.238 --> 00:07:04.160
I think your photophone paved the way for fiber optics, a technology we use today for all kinds

81
00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:05.080
of communication.

82
00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:08.001
Aha! I was correct then!

83
00:07:08.782 --> 00:07:11.564
Now I must tell you about my other inventions.

84
00:07:12.464 --> 00:07:16.567
Did you know I invented a telephonic probe, or metal detector?

85
00:07:17.428 --> 00:07:23.912
I invented it when President Garfield was shot in 1881 and doctors couldn't locate the bullet in his

86
00:07:23.972 --> 00:07:29.215
body. Sadly, I couldn't find the bullet, and poor Garfield died.

87
00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:33.238
Oh, I also designed kites and glides.

88
00:07:33.979 --> 00:07:37.468
Perhaps you've heard of the hydrofoil—a boat with airfoils?

89
00:07:38.489 --> 00:07:41.389
My final patent was for a very fast one!

90
00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:45.330
You were interested in a lot of different things!

91
00:07:46.191 --> 00:07:49.952
Before you go, would you like to see my modern telephone?

92
00:07:50.812 --> 00:07:52.032
It's pretty amazing!

93
00:07:52.873 --> 00:07:57.414
This might surprise you, Faye, but I lost some interest in the telephone over time.

94
00:07:58.494 --> 00:08:03.976
In fact, I refused to keep one in my study—too distracting for a busy scientist!

95
00:08:04.196 --> 00:08:05.476
So no, thank you.

96
00:08:06.472 --> 00:08:07.392
No problem!

97
00:08:07.753 --> 00:08:09.894
We'll let you get back to history now.

98
00:08:10.994 --> 00:08:20.918
Good-bye! Alexander Graham Bell never considered his inventions to be his most important achievements.

99
00:08:21.858 --> 00:08:27.981
Instead he believed teaching the deaf was the most meaningful job he ever had.

100
00:08:29.021 --> 00:08:30.562
He died at the age of 75 in 1922.

101
00:08:30.582 --> 00:08:40.732
On the day of his burial, every phone in America stayed silent for one minute to honor him.

102
00:08:41.813 --> 00:08:44.015
I hope you enjoyed today's interview.

103
00:08:44.555 --> 00:08:48.979
You've been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History!
